Overlay patch 0050: HANDS AT HEADSET RATE (VR R5, VR-DONOR-MAP 10's last
line -- "hand-matrix substitution by pointer identity ordered ahead of the
interpolation replacement map").

THE PROBLEM, which is R4's gap 5 and is a headset question R4 could not answer.
Overlay 0042 pins the two hand limbs to the controller pose, but it does so
during Play_Draw, which runs ONCE PER GAME TICK. LUS then re-executes that same
recorded display list for every intermediate frame with a per-frame matrix
REPLACEMENT MAP, blending the last two ticks' matrices. So the hands you see are
20 Hz of hand motion, interpolated, inside a 90 or 120 Hz frame: up to 50 ms of
lag and a stair-step no amount of filtering upstream can remove. The eyes
already run at compositor rate. The hands did not.

THE FIX, and it is the donor's: tag the hand's Mtx by POINTER at record time and
substitute the LIVE pose for it at interpretation time, ORDERED AHEAD OF the
interpolation map -- if a pointer is a hand's, the interpolated value is never
consulted. Since our 0031 interprets the whole list once per EYE and both eyes
of a pair run inside one compositor frame, that makes the hands step at
compositor rate for free, and keeps them coherent with the eyes because both
read the SAME pair slot (the R2a invariant: everything presented in a pair
carries one pose).

WHY A DELTA AND NOT A SUBSTITUTION. The hand limb's Mtx is not the hand pose --
it is scale * mirror * handPose, and the HELD ITEM draws on a second Mtx that is
itemOffset * scale * mirror * handPose. Replacing either outright would throw
away the local part. So we publish, alongside the pointers, the hand matrix that
was USED to build them (H_rec), and at interpretation compute

    D = inverse(H_rec) * H_live      and      M' = M * D

in row-vector convention, which is exactly right for both: (L * H_rec) * D
= L * H_live for any local L. The sword follows the hand for free, which is what
made 0042's "held items ride the hand matrix" true in the first place.

WHERE THE POINTERS COME FROM. `play->flexLimbOverrideMTX` is set by
SkelAnime_DrawFlexLimbOpa immediately BEFORE it calls the override, and points at
the Mtx slot this limb is about to be written into. So the override knows the
address without touching z_skelanime.c. The held item's Mtx comes from a
different allocation entirely (Matrix_NewMtx out of the graph pool, inside
PostLimbDraw), so instead of guessing it we TAG: the override sets
gSohVRHandMtxTag while it is pinning a hand, and Matrix_ToMtx -- the single
funnel every Mtx in the game passes through -- registers whatever is produced
while the tag is live. The tag is cleared on the next override call, and the draw
order (override -> the limb's own Mtx -> PostLimbDraw's item Mtx -> next limb's
override) is exactly the span that wants covering. Two allocations per hand is
the normal case; the table holds four.

THE ONE INVARIANT THIS MUST NOT BREAK. The eyes of a pair must see identical
hands. They do: the substitution reads gSohVRHandMat[gSohVRPairSlot], which 0031
latches once for the pair, and gSohVRPairSlot does not move between eye 0 and eye
1. It moves between PAIRS, which is precisely the rate we want.

FAILURE MODES, all of which fall back to exactly today's behaviour: the tag
table empty (nothing registered -- no VR, no hands, the override never ran);
gSohVRHandRecValid 0 (no record matrix, so no delta is computable); a
non-invertible record matrix (guarded; a zero-determinant basis returns
unchanged); gSohVRHandLive 0 (the red control -- `vr set handlive 0` reproduces
R4's 20 Hz hands for an A/B in the headset).

Two files: soh/src/code/sys_matrix.c (the registration funnel) and
libultraship/src/fast/interpreter.cpp (the substitution). z_player.c's tag
set/clear rides in overlay 0042's own regeneration, not here, because it lives
inside text 0042 inserted.

A NOTE ON THE SHAPE OF THE interpreter.cpp EDIT, because the first version of
this patch got it wrong and the failure was instructive: it originally SPLIT
GfxSpMatrix into a front half and a GfxSpMatrixApply back half. That is cleaner
C++ and it broke the overlay -- overlay 0031 also inserts into this function,
near its end, and the split moved 0031's context out from under its own reverse
probe, so `apply-overlay.sh` could no longer tell an applied 0031 from an
unapplied one and the second run failed. The shipped version adds ONE branch
ahead of the existing if/else and touches nothing else, which is the general
rule the spec already states for stacked patches: prefer the edit that leaves
every other patch's context alone, even when it reads slightly worse.

